INT. TIDE-CLOCK TOWER - INSIDE A WHALE SKELETON - DUSK

A cathedral of bone. Ribs arch overhead like the hull of an overturned ship, strung with lanterns and rope ladders. At the center, a vast brass clock whose pendulum is a swinging glass bell filled with seawater. Each tick sloshes a silver tide across the floor.

BRAMWELL TOLLIVER, 70s, tiny and bent, in a coat patched with sailcloth, oils a gear with a feather. A brass monocle sits on his nose, one lens fogged.

The great doors boom open. LADY SERAPHINE VANE, 40s, strides in, sea-green cloak dragging a wake of damp, silver rings on every finger. Behind her trots PIP, a clockwork heron, tall as a child, copper beak clicking.

BRAMWELL
You're late, Lady Vane. The tide has turned twice.

SERAPHINE
The tide turns when I command it, Bramwell.

BRAMWELL
Not in my tower.

Pip tips her head and clicks. Seraphine sets a gloved hand on the heron's neck, and the clicking softens.

SERAPHINE
Pip, quiet. He is an old man and he is frightened.

BRAMWELL
I am not frightened. I am furious.

PIP
(a thin, whistling voice)
Furious is frightened wearing boots.

Bramwell laughs despite himself, a dry bark. The pendulum swings. A silver tide washes over their feet and recedes.

SERAPHINE
I have come to take the Moonweight, Bramwell.

BRAMWELL
The Moonweight holds the tide in its place.

SERAPHINE
The tide has held long enough. The sea is tired of being told when to rise.

BRAMWELL
And when it rises with no hand upon it? What then, Lady?

SERAPHINE
Then it rises. And we float.

Bramwell lays the feather down on the gear. He crosses to a small iron door in a rib, thumbs a latch, and takes out a lead-grey disc the size of a dinner plate. It hums.

BRAMWELL
Forty years I tended this. My wife is buried under that floor.

SERAPHINE
I know.

BRAMWELL
You know. And still you ask.

SERAPHINE
I do not ask, Bramwell. I came out of courtesy.

PIP
Courtesy is a polite word for a knife.

SERAPHINE
Pip.

PIP
I only quote.

Bramwell weighs the disc in both hands. The pendulum bell slows. A lantern gutters. Somewhere high in the ribs, a gull cries.

BRAMWELL
You were a girl when you first came up those stairs. Do you remember what you asked me?

SERAPHINE
I asked if the whale was still alive.

BRAMWELL
And I said?

SERAPHINE
That it was only sleeping.

BRAMWELL
Was I lying?

SERAPHINE
(softly)
No.

She holds out both hands. Bramwell looks at the Moonweight, then at her, and his shoulders drop.

BRAMWELL
Take it. Let the sea wake what it must.

Seraphine receives the disc. Its hum deepens. The silver tide climbs slowly up the ribs, quieter now, and the great bell comes to rest.

PIP
Is that the last tick?

BRAMWELL
For tonight, small one. For tonight.

Seraphine bows her head. The lanterns dim one by one.
